<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in My Classroom – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Express feelings and basic needs using words and body language.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use drawings or gestures to help classmates understand classroom items or colors (e.g., pointing to a red crayon or drawing a chair when asked what they need).<br>Can express a basic classroom need using body language or words (e.g., pointing to a pencil when they need one or saying "book" while reaching for it).<br>Can show understanding of color-related instructions through gestures or drawings (e.g., picking up the correct color when asked "Find something blue," or coloring a square red when shown a red object).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Color and Object Scavenger Hunt!" The teacher hides flashcards of classroom objects (pencil, book, crayon, chair) and color spots (red, blue, yellow) around the room. Students stand up and look for one specific item/color when called out (e.g., "Find something RED!"). They point to it or pick it up. This revisits vocabulary and encourages movement (TPR).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simple mediation through gestures and pointing. For example, the teacher pretends to look for a "blue crayon" and sighs. A student might point. The teacher says "Blue crayon! Thank you!" Teacher then models helping another child: "Maya needs a pencil. Where is a pencil?" (Teacher points to a pencil and gives it to Maya). The teacher emphasizes using gestures (pointing, reaching) and simple words (pencil, book, red, blue) to communicate needs or help others. Teacher models pronouncing /p/ in "pencil" and /b/ in "book" clearly.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a red crayon) "Is this blue?" (Students shake head or say "No!") "What color is it?" (Students say "Red!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a chair) "Is this a book?" (Students shake head or say "No!") "What is this?" (Students say "Chair!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in My Classroom".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards of classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, crayon, board, table, bag, desk) and color swatches (red, blue, yellow). Realia (actual classroom objects) if available. A simple storybook or large picture with labeled classroom items and colors.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying classroom objects and colors: Responding to "What is this?" by naming familiar items (e.g., "pencil") and "What color is this?" by naming familiar colors (e.g., "red"). Following directions: Pointing to or retrieving items based on instructions (e.g., "Pick up the book.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large picture of a classroom. "Look at our classroom! What do you see?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to items and asks, "What is this?" (e.g., "pencil"). Students respond chorally or individually. "What color is it?" (e.g., "red").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher introduces simple sentences for needs: (Teacher points to a pencil) "I need a pencil." (Teacher points to a book) "I need a book." Students repeat using gestures.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher uses Yes/No questions and prepositions: "Is the book on the table?" (pointing) "Yes!" or "No, it's under the table." (Students use TPR to show understanding).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the red crayon." (Teacher observes students' actions).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me something blue." (Students hold up a blue item or point).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a pencil) "Do I need a book?" (Students gesture no, or say "No!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Yes/No questions ("Is this your pencil?"), Prepositions ("The book is on the table."), WH-questions ("What color is it?").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag, red, blue, yellow, on, under, in, next to.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /p/ in "pencil," /b/ in "book." Phonemic awareness: Initial sounds: /t/ in "chair," /f/ in "short."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Identifying classroom objects and colors. Following directions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Expressing gratitude ("thank you").</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Whisper, Draw, and Guess" (Controlled Mediation)</b><br>
            Students sit in small groups. The teacher whispers an object or color (e.g., "red crayon") to one student. That student silently draws it on a mini whiteboard or paper. The other students in the group guess what it is, using simple questions like "Is it red?" "Is it a crayon?" The first student to guess correctly gets to draw next. This activity encourages non-verbal and simple verbal mediation. Teacher monitors pronunciation of /r/, /c/, /p/.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Help My Friend Find It" (Semi-controlled Mediation)</b><br>
            Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of classroom object flashcards and color flashcards. One student secretly chooses an object and its color (e.g., "blue book"). The other student asks simple questions, "Is it a book?" (partner points to book flashcard) "Is it blue?" (partner points to blue color card or nods/shakes head). The student uses gestures and single words to help their partner understand. Teacher encourages "thank you" when a partner helps. Teacher prompts for /b/ in "book".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "Classroom Needs Circle" (Pair Interaction/Peer Check)</b><br>
            Students sit in a circle. The teacher gives each student a classroom item (pencil, crayon, small book). One student pretends to need something different (e.g., "I need a blue crayon" while holding a red pencil). Other students in the circle, or a designated "helper," use gestures (pointing to a blue crayon, reaching for it) or simple words ("blue") to help the student get the correct item. The student who needed help says "Thank you!" Teacher supports preposition use: "Is the crayon on the floor?" or "It's next to the book." Focus on initial /t/ in "table."</li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in My Classroom".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative project work (creating a classroom poster) with integrated mediation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Our Colorful Classroom Poster" (21st Century Skills Project)</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 children). Each group receives a large piece of paper and crayons (red, blue, yellow) and a few picture cutouts of classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, desk).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to create a "Color and Label My Classroom" poster. They will glue the object cutouts onto the poster and color them with the appropriate colors.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Integration:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student might need a specific color crayon for their drawing (e.g., "I need a blue crayon"). They can point to a blue object in the room or a blue color swatch to show their friend what they need. Their friend mediates by understanding and passing the crayon.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student might be unsure which color to use for a "red book" cutout. A peer can point to a red object in the classroom, or show a red flashcard, to help them understand.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can point to their drawing/coloring and ask, "Is this red?" Their peers can affirm or correct with gestures or simple words.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, prompting mediation: "Carlos, Ana needs the red crayon. Can you help her?" "Point to the chair for Mateo on your poster."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The goal is for students to collaboratively create a colorful poster, practicing using gestures and simple words to explain, ask for, and help understand classroom items and colors in the context of the project.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer-mediated check.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their "Colorful Classroom Poster." Students individually take turns pointing to an object or color on their poster and attempting to name it (e.g., "Red crayon!" or "Chair!"). The teacher will also prompt individual students to mediate for a peer (e.g., "Diego, can you help Sofia find the blue crayon on the poster?").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Uses clear /p/ in "pencil," /b/ in "book," and initial /t/ in "table."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Identifies and names 3+ classroom items/colors (pencil, book, crayon, red, blue, yellow).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Gestures/Drawings for Mediation:</b> Uses pointing, reaching, or simple drawings to help peers understand needs or identify items/colors.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Sociolinguistic:</b> Actively participates in group and uses "thank you" when receiving help or materials.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Understanding of Color Instructions:</b> Picks up or points to the correct color/object when instructed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will be shown two large picture cards: one with a happy face helping a friend find a red crayon, and another with a sad face not understanding. Students point to the picture that shows if they felt they could help a friend or understand when a friend helped them today. Alternatively, "Thumbs up if you helped a friend today! Thumbs down if it was hard."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I help my friend find a color for our poster today?" (Students can nod/shake head or point to a happy/sad face).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I show my friend what I needed (e.g., a crayon) using my hands or words?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of mediation (gestures, simple words, pointing) did students use most effectively? Which were challenging for Pre-K learners?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the instructions for the poster project clear enough to allow for natural mediation opportunities? What adjustments could improve collaboration and communication?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students integrate the target vocabulary (classroom objects, colors, prepositions) into their mediation efforts?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What scaffolds (e.g., more visual cues, teacher modeling) are needed for future mediation tasks with this age group?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Colors in My Classroom" theme and the "Color and Label My Classroom" project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on students' emergent communicative skills and their ability to express basic needs and understanding through mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>